-- | Making the journal non-optional, using Daml's authorization model.
--
-- THE HOLE EVERYTHING ELSE SAT ON. Journal.daml and Attempt.daml both ended
-- with the same admission: nothing forces an application to route through
-- them. An application with a second path to the same asset bypasses all of
-- it, and no module it imports can see that. Every other guarantee here was
-- conditional on the application cooperating, which is not a guarantee.
--
-- Honest version of what that meant: an auditor asking "can I trust this
-- record" was being answered with "if the application chose to write it".
--
-- THE FIX IS NOT A CONVENTION. It is Daml's authorization model.
--
-- In Daml, a consuming choice needs the authority of the contract's
-- signatories. A party's authority is only available inside a transaction
-- where that party is a signatory of a contract being exercised, or an
-- explicit controller.
--
-- So: put a `guardian` party on the ASSET as a signatory, and make the
-- guardian's authority reachable ONLY from inside an attempt resolution.
-- Then an application that tries to move the asset any other way does not
-- have the authority and the ledger refuses it. Not discouraged. Refused.
--
--     template Wallet
--       with owner : Party; guardian : Party; balance : Decimal
--       where signatory owner, guardian        -- <- the whole fix
--
-- The guardian is a signatory of `AttemptJournal`, so its authority flows
-- into `Open`. `Open` creates an `Attempt` that also carries the guardian as
-- a signatory, which is what carries that authority into the SECOND
-- transaction, where the work happens. Exercise `SettleAttempt` and the body
-- has owner, actor and guardian authority, enough to consume the asset.
-- Exercise anything on the asset outside that, and you have owner authority
-- and nothing else.
--
-- WHAT AN APPLICATION HAS TO DO. Implement `Journalled` on whatever it wants
-- journalled, and name the guardian as a signatory of it. That is the whole
-- integration. The work itself stays entirely the application's: `perform`
-- is its own code, and this module never sees inside it.
--
-- WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, and it is a smaller hole than the one it closes.
-- An application can still create assets that do NOT name the guardian, and
-- those are outside the journal. But that is now visible in the template
-- definition rather than hidden in a code path: a reviewer reads the
-- `signatory` line and knows immediately. The question moves from "audit
-- every call site" to "read one line per template", which is the difference
-- between an audit that is possible and one that is not.
--
-- The guardian party is also a dependency. If its participant is down,
-- nothing moves. That is the cost of the guarantee and it should be a
-- deliberate choice, not a surprise.
module Guarded where

-- | What the guard needs to know about a thing it guards.
data JournalledView = JournalledView
  with
    owner : Party
      -- ^ Whose asset this is.
    guardian : Party
      -- ^ The journal's party. MUST be a signatory of the implementing
      -- template, or the guarantee is not there. `assertGuarded` checks it.
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Implemented by anything an application wants to be unable to move
-- without a journal entry.
interface Journalled where
  viewtype JournalledView

  -- | The application's own work. This module never looks inside it and does
  -- not want to: the point is that WHEN it runs is constrained, not what it
  -- does.
  perform : Update ()

  -- | The only way the work runs. Controlled by the guardian as well as the
  -- owner, so it cannot be exercised outside a context that carries guardian
  -- authority, and the only such context is an attempt resolution.
  choice Perform : ()
    controller (view this).owner, (view this).guardian
    do perform this

-- | Fails unless the guardian really is a signatory of the implementing
-- contract.
--
-- Naming the guardian in the view while leaving it off the `signatory` line
-- would produce something that looks guarded, passes a glance, and is not.
-- Called on every resolution, so a template wired up wrongly fails the first
-- time it is used rather than silently never being enforced.
assertGuarded : ContractId Journalled -> Update ()
assertGuarded cid = do
  guarded <- fetch cid
  let v = view guarded
  signatories <- signatory <$> pure guarded
  assertMsg
    "the guardian is not a signatory of this contract, so it is not guarded"
    (v.guardian `elem` signatories)
